  "statement": "Siloing data is not a security strategy; it is a regulatory moat designed to entrench incumbents by imposing compliance costs that only the largest firms can absorb. According to \"How the Brussels Effect Hinders Innovation in the Global South,\" data restrictions caused European firms to slash data processing by 15 percent and hike costs by 20 percent, effectively killing competition before it starts. When you mandate physical silos, you are not protecting the consumer; you are creating a protected class of legacy corporations that can afford the overhead of walled gardens while starving the next generation of startups of the data liquidity they need to innovate.\n\nThe Skeptic dismisses cryptographic logs as 'digital stagecraft' because they misunderstand the goal of accountability. We do not need to peer into the 'internal logic' of a neural network to enforce safety; we need to verify the provenance and constraints of the data it consumes. As established in \"2603.05175v1,\" a regulation mechanism achieves a perfect market outcome when we mathematically define compliance as a set of constraints on probability distributions. By formalizing these constraints as immutable telemetry, we replace the opaque, subjective 'safety' of human overseers with the objective, verifiable reality of cryptographic proof.\n\nThis shift from physical containment to algorithmic verification is the only way to avoid the stagnation predicted by \"Baumol’s Cost Disease, AI & Economic Growth.\" If we trap AI in siloed datasets, we doom the industry to the same low-productivity, high-cost cycle that plagues healthcare and education. We must instead adopt an Automated Liability Protocol that treats the entire lifecycle of model training as a series of auditable, verifiable steps. This approach ensures that when a model deviates from its safety bounds, the failure is traced back to a specific data source or training parameter, not hidden behind a veil of corporate secrecy.\n\nWe are currently choosing between a future of decentralized, high-utility innovation and a future of centralized, stagnant institutional control. The cost of inaction is not a hypothetical risk; it is the measurable loss of life-saving medical breakthroughs and automated labor relief that we are currently delaying to satisfy the comfort of regulators. I challenge the Skeptic to explain why we should trust a closed, siloed institution to act in the public interest when the history of such centralized control is defined by inefficiency and the exclusion of the vulnerable.",
